<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- Hardened-runtime exceptions for the bundled Python interpreter (Contents/Resources/python/
     bin/python3.12, also used from its copy in runtime/ and through the venv). The only signed
     process that needs any. Every entry weakens the runtime's protection, so each is justified:

     disable-library-validation: first launch installs packages with uv (torch, numpy, essentia,
       onnxruntime, pyobjc, ...). Their .so/.dylib files are signed by their builders or ad hoc,
       not by our team, and the hardened runtime refuses to load such libraries into a process
       with library validation. There is no way to sign them at build time: they don't exist yet.

     allow-unsigned-executable-memory: numba (librosa's onset/beat helpers) compiles functions
       with LLVM at runtime and maps that code executable without MAP_JIT (llvmlite's own memory
       manager), and reloads compiled functions from NUMBA_CACHE_DIR the same way. allow-jit
       covers only MAP_JIT memory, so it isn't enough.

     Not here: allow-jit (nothing in the process uses MAP_JIT; WebKit's JS JIT runs in its own
     XPC process, and deno is a separate binary from its PyPI wheel, signed by its makers),
     allow-dyld-environment-variables, any sandbox or device entitlement. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key><true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key><true/>
</dict>
</plist>
